<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasselt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasselt is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the capital of the Flemish province of Limburg, on the Demer. A town of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>, it passed with the county to the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Liège in 1366 and became one of their 'good towns'. It grew as the provincial capital in the 19th century and is known for jenever distilling.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasselt.jpg" alt="Hasselt" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thebeautyparlorisfilledwithsailors · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Hasselt-_stadscentrum_met_Sint_Quintinuskathedraal_en_Blauwe_Boulevard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hasselt lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Campine</a> region, north of the Demer, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a> region to the south. The name derives from the Germanic word Hasaluth, which refers to the common hazel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hasselt was founded in about the 7th century on the Helbeek, a tributary of the Demer. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>, whose borders were roughly those of the present province of Limburg. It is first named in a document in 1165. In 1232 Arnold IV, Count of Loon, gave the city the same freedoms as those enjoyed in Liège. From 1366 the county was ruled directly by the Prince-Bishopric of Liège, until annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1794.</p><p>Under the First French Empire Maastricht was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Lower Meuse. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1815 the area became part of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands, and King William I renamed the department after the medieval Duchy of Limburg, a duchy that had never included Hasselt or Maastricht. Belgium split from the Netherlands in 1830, but the status of Limburg was settled only in 1839, when it was divided into Belgian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> parts. Hasselt became the provisional capital of the Belgian province. In ecclesiastical terms Belgian Limburg became independent of the Diocese of Liège only in 1967, when Hasselt became the seat of a new diocese.</p><p>Hasselt is known for jenever, a gin. It escaped the ban on the production and sale of the drink imposed in 1601 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-vii-archduke-of-austria">Albert VII, Archduke of Austria</a>, and Isabella Clara Eugenia, because it belonged to the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. Jenever production was the most important industry in Limburg in the 19th century, with most distilleries in Hasselt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hasselt is the capital and largest city of the province of Limburg in the Flemish Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the seat of the Catholic diocese of Hasselt. The Gothic St Quentin's Cathedral, begun in the 11th century, and the Refuge of Herkenrode Abbey, of 1542, the city's oldest civic building, stand there, and Hasselt University was established in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasselt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Loon was largely rural, with small towns such as <b>Hasselt</b> and Borgloon.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-loon">County of Loon</a></li></ul></section>
